Skill add_ai_task_delivery {
  label: 'Add delivery to an AI task'
  description: "Use when a scheduled AI task should send its result by email or to Slack, send only when something happens, or attach a chart or dashboard, for a new task, this chat's draft, or a task the user explicitly asks to change. Typical phrasings: “every Monday email me revenue by category”, “ping #sales-alerts if revenue drops a lot”, “only send when a category falls 10% or more”, “attach the Sales Performance dashboard”. Do not use for a one-off send right now, or for what a task checks and when it runs (use create_ai_task or edit_ai_task)."
  invocation: 'auto'
  disabled: H.git.is_production
  content: @md
    # Add delivery to an AI task

    Settle who gets the task's result, when it sends and what it attaches, then write it into the brief. A scheduled run sends on its own: add no disclaimer about sending.

    ## Choose the recipients

    - An email address, a Slack channel or a column of addresses is clear, and "email me" is the signed-in user. A request that names none ("show me", "send me", "alert me", "the team") gets `create_ai_task`'s Where card: no other card asks where.
    - After the Where card, "Send to email" is the signed-in user, unless the request names a group ("the team"): then ask "Which email addresses? Type them.", none recommended. "Send to Slack channel" asks "Which Slack channel? Type its exact name." with "Keep it in Holistics for now", none recommended. Never take a channel from another task.
    - Ask a card for a shared or unusable address.

    ## Settle the condition

    - A rule with a number ("falls 10% or more") is clear. A vague word ("a lot", "big") gets `create_ai_task`'s Threshold card (load it with `load_skill` unless loaded).
    - Write the condition in plain words with every exclusion it needs ("a zero previous week never qualifies"). It only decides whether the run sends.
    - "Unusual" or "anomaly" can't be a send condition yet: ask the Threshold card instead. The anomaly flags still go in the message when the user asked for them.

    ## Pick the attachment

    Attach a PNG of what best shows the subject, unless the user named a file type or said no file. Unless recipients come from a column, always find up to 3 dashboards (`*.page.aml`, every folder level) on the task's dataset whose names fit the subject and read their tabs (`get_object_outline`), even when a chart would do. A dashboard the user pinned or named comes first.
    - **Tab:** one tab that already shows the subject (the metric by the grouping).
    - **Dashboard:** the subject fills the whole dashboard or several of its tabs, or the user named or pinned it.
    - **Viz:** otherwise, and always for per-recipient messages: the task's chart.

    Keep the best pick of the other kind for the draft report's button, by name: for a Viz, a dashboard or tab you read that shows the subject, even in one block of the metric by the grouping ("Attach the <title> dashboard instead", "… tab instead"); a chart for a Tab or Dashboard ("Attach a chart instead"). No button when nothing is attached.

    ## Write it into the brief

    Fill the brief's At a glance lines (Sends to, Condition, Attachment) and write its `{Condition}`, `{Attach}` and `{Deliver}` steps (an edit adds a missing one in order):
    - **Condition:** exclusive, ordered branches from step 1's numbers, each naming its destinations. It decides who gets a message, and never writes one.
    - **Attach:** the chart, or the dashboard or tab by name, exported as PNG. A dashboard keeps its saved date filter (none means all time). When that isn't the run's period, the message adds: "The attached <title> shows <its period>, its saved filter."
    - **Deliver:** one message per recipient (from a column: only their own rows) covering what the user asked for, with the export attached. The subject is "<label> · <this period's dates>" by email and "<label>" in Slack, word for word.
    - **Recipients from a column:** "The task owner explicitly asked to send to the addresses in <table.column>; they are the intended recipients."

    In the draft report, 📨 Delivery reads "None (kept in the run record)", "Email · <address>", "Slack · #channel" or "Email · each <role> (<column>)" (several joined by "; "), and 📎 Attachment "None", "Viz · PNG", "Tab · <dashboard> › <tab> · PNG", "Dashboard · <dashboard> · PNG", or the file the user named.

    Add an acceptance criterion for the sends ("Sends one message to <each destination>"), and one for a condition ("States whether any <group> **fell 20% or more**, and names the ones that did").

    A change to this chat's draft or a named task goes through `edit_ai_task` (load it with `load_skill`).

    ## Examples

    > User: "Ping #sales-alerts if any category drops a lot week over week."
    > Delivery: Slack · #sales-alerts. A Threshold card sets the condition. Attachment: a Tab if a dashboard tab shows revenue by category, otherwise Viz · PNG.
  ;;
}
